WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:18.299
Hi, I'm Toby and welcome to the Joy of Mathematics, I'm so glad you could join me today.

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:24.990
I'm going to run some ideas across the bottom of the screen and these are ideas which we will come to understand today.

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:29.579
It's another beautiful day outside today, although it is a little windy.

00:00:29.579 --> 00:00:32.759
I've gone ahead and already drawn a tree for us

00:00:33.160 --> 00:00:40.349
but a tree is a little bit like an iceberg in that what you can see on the surface is only a small part of

00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:41.320
the story.

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:47.099
What is going to be really important to us today is what we can see under ground, a

00:00:49.690 --> 00:00:52.320
tree could not exist without its roots.

00:00:53.170 --> 00:00:58.769
Now last time I saw you we were talking about logarithms and how they can be used to rewrite

00:00:59.230 --> 00:01:05.400
exponents, one particularly popular exponent is the square, a number times itself.

00:01:06.940 --> 00:01:10.829
We could have 3 times 3 is equal to 9

00:01:11.710 --> 00:01:14.699
4 times 4 which is 16

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:17.940
or even 5 times 5

00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:20.580
which is 25.

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:23.789
These are all squares and could be written as

00:01:24.550 --> 00:01:26.380
3 squared

00:01:26.380 --> 00:01:27.760
4 squared

00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:28.930
or

00:01:28.930 --> 00:01:35.819
5 squared but what if some leaves carried by the wind were to come across and land

00:01:36.370 --> 00:01:38.370
right here

00:01:39.460 --> 00:01:41.640
and right here so

00:01:43.390 --> 00:01:46.050
that all we could see was the result.

00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:52.529
We knew that something squared was equal to 16, but we couldn't quite remember what.

00:01:53.050 --> 00:01:57.300
Well, what we would need to do would be to find the square root of

00:01:57.820 --> 00:01:58.900
16.

00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:01.139
Roots are what allow a tree to grow

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:08.189
the better the roots the more nutrients they can collect and the more support they can provide.

00:02:09.610 --> 00:02:15.449
Just like in life having support can make it feel easier to achieve great things,

00:02:16.420 --> 00:02:21.570
although support can often be hard to come by or difficult to recognize.

00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:33.699
Just drawing in our roots here.

00:02:45.810 --> 00:02:51.949
You can see these roots would be very good at providing some moral and

00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:54.980
structural support to the tree.

00:02:58.530 --> 00:03:04.489
We also forgot to add the apple to our tree before so we can go ahead and sneak that in now.

00:03:06.810 --> 00:03:13.699
Now in these square roots there are 16 units of dirt, that's the area of the square.

00:03:14.370 --> 00:03:16.370
The area of a square is

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:19.430
calculated from its base times its height and

00:03:19.950 --> 00:03:24.349
since these are the same, if we know the length of just one side,

00:03:24.690 --> 00:03:30.769
we know the square root. We can go ahead and divide this up into a grid

00:03:33.540 --> 00:03:34.889
and

00:03:34.889 --> 00:03:37.039
if the length of each

00:03:37.829 --> 00:03:43.909
side was 3 then we would have 9 units of dirt.

00:03:44.549 --> 00:03:50.659
But if instead it was 5 on each side, we would have 25 units of dirt.

00:03:51.449 --> 00:03:57.019
But the sweet spot for us and you're going to see it here, is that if each

00:03:57.810 --> 00:03:59.370
length

00:03:59.370 --> 00:04:07.370
of the roots, each side of the square is 4 units long then in total we will have 16 units of dirt

00:04:07.650 --> 00:04:15.169
sitting right here in our roots, and that's exactly what we wanted. So we know that the square root of 16

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:18.379
will be equal to 4.

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:22.789
All these numbers that I've mentioned 9, 16, 25,

00:04:23.849 --> 00:04:29.569
they're all called perfect squares because they are the squares of whole numbers.

00:04:30.090 --> 00:04:37.489
We could look at a non-perfect square say 20, although all numbers are perfect in their own way and

00:04:38.039 --> 00:04:42.379
we could look, well 20 is between 16 and 25

00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:50.840
so the square root of 20 would be somewhere between 4 and 5 and in fact, it is 4.47.

00:04:51.599 --> 00:04:58.609
Now there's actually another solution to all of these square roots, and I hate to mention it as an afterthought

00:04:58.820 --> 00:05:04.490
because nobody likes being an afterthought, but we could have also had for example

00:05:05.550 --> 00:05:12.289
-4 times -4 being equal to 16 because two wrongs make a right, but

00:05:12.570 --> 00:05:17.869
in our case having a negative side length wouldn't make a lot of sense

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:21.469
so we were fine to just take the positive solution.

00:05:21.540 --> 00:05:28.490
There is actually a symbol that tells us to look underground and find the square root of a number and

00:05:28.830 --> 00:05:30.830
that is this

00:05:30.990 --> 00:05:37.550
heart beat kind of symbol here called the radical because it is pretty radically cool.

00:05:38.070 --> 00:05:40.070
Now I remember when I very first

00:05:40.410 --> 00:05:43.010
encountered the symbol on some math homework at school

00:05:43.710 --> 00:05:48.439
but I was pretty confused because I had never seen it before I didn't know what it was called

00:05:48.570 --> 00:05:51.710
and I didn't know how to find out.

00:05:51.930 --> 00:05:57.680
I wanted to google it but I didn't know how to describe it in the Google search, do you type in

00:05:58.050 --> 00:06:00.649
'tick like symbol' or 'heartbeat'?

00:06:00.650 --> 00:06:03.170
I kind of liked the name heartbeat symbol.

00:06:03.510 --> 00:06:04.700
Now if you like me

00:06:04.700 --> 00:06:08.839
you might be a little bit curious as to the root of this square root symbol

00:06:08.840 --> 00:06:14.000
and there seems to be a little bit of disagreement about where exactly it comes from

00:06:14.490 --> 00:06:21.110
one story that I've heard as to its origin is that it comes from the Arabic word for root and

00:06:21.390 --> 00:06:25.669
it comes from the first letter of the word which looks a little bit

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:28.640
like this.

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:37.219
The Latin word for root is actually radix and it comes from radix quadratum

00:06:37.230 --> 00:06:39.980
which actually means the side of a square.

00:06:40.530 --> 00:06:42.739
Some mathematicians wrote this as

00:06:44.700 --> 00:06:46.700
Capital R x.

00:06:47.160 --> 00:06:54.860
Now that might have made its way to becoming the modern symbol or it might have also come from a lowercase r

00:06:55.200 --> 00:07:01.460
also for radix, but there's one more possible origin story and that is that some

00:07:02.100 --> 00:07:06.170
mathematicians used to denote a square root as putting a

00:07:06.750 --> 00:07:11.239
dot or a few dots above the numbers that they wanted to take the square root of

00:07:11.490 --> 00:07:15.180
and it's thought that maybe somewhere along the way these dots

00:07:15.490 --> 00:07:19.530
just acquired a bit of an upstroke and that became our symbol.

00:07:20.139 --> 00:07:22.139
What we do know is that in

00:07:22.900 --> 00:07:24.130
1637

00:07:24.130 --> 00:07:30.749
Descartes was the first to use the square root symbol with this line over the top that has a name

00:07:30.750 --> 00:07:37.199
It's called the vinculum and that includes everybody in the square root who was supposed to be there.

00:07:37.240 --> 00:07:44.099
It's like a big group hug. The square root symbol typically means to just take the principal or the positive

00:07:44.289 --> 00:07:49.889
result of the square root. So there is only one leaf that would satisfy

00:07:50.440 --> 00:07:53.160
the square root of 16 is equal to

00:07:54.460 --> 00:07:56.289
leaf.

00:07:56.289 --> 00:08:02.519
Only one solution would satisfy that but if instead we were to have leaf

00:08:04.659 --> 00:08:07.649
squared is equal to 16

00:08:07.960 --> 00:08:13.530
there are two leafs that would satisfy this and we would have to write our answer as being

00:08:17.110 --> 00:08:19.110
plus or minus

00:08:19.180 --> 00:08:20.349
4.

00:08:20.349 --> 00:08:24.299
The square root can also just as easily be written as

00:08:24.880 --> 00:08:29.549
16 to the half, we can start using fractional powers and

00:08:30.250 --> 00:08:32.880
for other roots say the cube root

00:08:32.890 --> 00:08:38.369
we will just extend this to be 16 to the one-third or we could write

00:08:38.649 --> 00:08:44.189
3 in our little square root symbol, and that would give us a different solution.

00:08:47.529 --> 00:08:52.949
Squaring any number positive or negative will give us a positive result.

00:08:53.890 --> 00:09:01.799
Squaring 0 will give us 0 so there is actually no way for a negative number to have any real root.

00:09:02.350 --> 00:09:09.270
But we can make things more inclusive if we invite over our imaginary friends, if we have

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:12.000
we introduce our friend i squared

00:09:12.850 --> 00:09:15.390
to be equal to minus 1

00:09:15.910 --> 00:09:17.910
then we would have that i

00:09:18.110 --> 00:09:21.110
is equal to the square root of -1.

00:09:22.200 --> 00:09:29.209
Technically - i would also be equal to the square root of -1, but by convention we just take positive

00:09:29.279 --> 00:09:33.859
i to be our square root minus ,1 our principal root.

00:09:34.200 --> 00:09:39.079
Then if we wanted to do the square root of minus 16

00:09:39.300 --> 00:09:44.209
we could do that and it would give us the square root of

00:09:44.910 --> 00:09:48.469
16 times the square root of -1

00:09:49.230 --> 00:09:52.070
leading us to an answer of 4i.

00:09:53.730 --> 00:09:58.760
Moral of the story is don't let anyone tell you that you can't have a square root.

00:09:58.760 --> 00:10:03.169
You can even take the square root of an imaginary number

00:10:03.660 --> 00:10:07.070
but you can see my other video for how to do that.

00:10:07.589 --> 00:10:11.989
So that has been our trip underground to understand square roots

00:10:12.180 --> 00:10:19.130
if you haven't already then take a walk near some trees and try to imagine what their hidden roots look like.

00:10:19.380 --> 00:10:23.299
I'd like to wish you happy studying and I hope that you have an

00:10:23.910 --> 00:10:25.910
absolutely

00:10:26.070 --> 00:10:28.070
mathematical day!

00:10:52.499 --> 00:10:54.499
 

